  "text": "לכאורה, נַםואל, we need to find the points of intersection between the parabola \\( x = 34y^2 \\) and the piecewise linear function \\( f(x) \\).\n\nfirstly, we need to consider the piecewise linear function. The function is defined as:\n\\[ f(x) = \\begin{cases} \nx & \\text{if } x \\in [-1, 1) \\\\\n2 - x & \\text{if } x \\in [1, 3)\n\\end{cases} \\]\n\nThe function is periodic with period 4, i.e., \\( f(x+4) = f(x) \\).\n\nwe need to find the points where the parabola \\( x = 34y^2 \\) intersects the piecewise linear function. we will do this by considering the two cases of the piecewise function separately.\n\n### Case 1: \\( x \\in [-1, 1) \\)\n\\[ f(x) = x \\]\n\\[ x = 34y^2 \\]\n\\[ y^2 = \\frac{x}{34} \\]\n\\[ y = \\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{x}{34}} \\]\n\nwe need to check if \\( x \\in [-1, 1) \\):\n\\[ -1 \\leq x < 1 \\]\n\\[ -1 \\leq \\frac{x}{34} < 1 \\]\n\\[ -34 \\leq x < 34 \\]\n\nsince \\( x \\in [-1, 1) \\), we have:\n\\[ -34 \\leq x < 34 \\]\n\\[ -34 \\leq \\frac{x}{34} < 34 \\]\n\\[ -34 \\leq y^2 < 34 \\]\n\\[ -\\sqrt{34} \\leq y < \\sqrt{34} \\]\n\nsince \\( y \\) is a coordinate of intersection points, we need to check the range of \\( y \\) values that satisfy the intersection condition:\n\\[ -\\sqrt{34} \\leq y < \\sqrt{34} \\]\n\n### Case 2: \\( x \\in [1, 3) \\)\n\\[ f(x) = 2 - x \\]\n\\[ x = 34y^2 \\]\n\\[ y^2 = \\frac{x}{34} \\]\n\\[ y = \\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{x}{34}} \\]\n\nwe need to check if \\( x \\in [1, 3) \\):\n\\[ 1 \\leq x < 3 \\]\n\\[ 1 \\leq \\frac{x}{34} < 3 \\]\n\\[ 34 \\leq x < 102 \\]\n\nsince \\( x \\in [1, 3) \\), we have:\n\\[ 34 \\leq x < 102 \\]\n\\[ 34 \\leq \\frac{x}{34} < 102 \\]\n\\[ 34 \\leq y^2 < 102 \\]\n\\[ \\sqrt{34} \\leq y < \\sqrt{102} \\]\n\nsince \\( y \\) is a coordinate of intersection points, we need to check the range of \\( y \\) values that satisfy the intersection condition:\n\\[ \\sqrt{34} \\leq y < \\sqrt{102} \\]\n\n### Sum of the \\( y \\)-coordinates\nThe \\( y \\)-coordinates of the intersection points are:\n\\[ y_1 = \\sqrt{\\frac{x_1}{34}}, \\quad y_2 = -\\sqrt{\\frac{x_1}{34}}, \\quad y_3 = \\sqrt{\\frac{x_2}{34}}, \\quad y_4 = -\\sqrt{\\frac{x_2}{34}}, \\quad y_5 = \\sqrt{\\frac{x_3}{34}}, \\quad y_6 = -\\sqrt{\\frac{x_3}{34}}, \\ldots \\]\n\nThe sum of the \\( y \\)-coordinates is:\n\\[ \\left( \\sqrt{\\frac{x_1}{34}} - \\sqrt{\\frac{x_1}{34}} \\right) + \\left( \\sqrt{\\frac{x_2}{34}} - \\sqrt{\\frac{x_2}{34}} \\right) + \\cdots \\]\n\\[ = 0 + 0 + \\cdots \\]\n\nThe sum of the \\( y \\)-coordinates is zero.\n\nTherefore, the sum of the \\( y \\)-coordinates of the intersection points is:\n\\[ \\boxed{0} \\]",
